index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..253234a2fc3255201fdfea61bca0f509afd82bad
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,171 @@
+# Polynomial Bézout witnesses from arXiv:2604.18991
+
+| profile | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B** — an efficient reference algorithm is disclosed and measured |
+| Native domain | `number_theory` |
+| Object regime | `rational_exact` |
+| Computational core | `polynomial_identity` |
+| Certificate | `polynomial` |
+| Intuition | `invariant`: recognize an (E)-th-root-of-unity quotient |
+| Domain essentiality | `native`; no reduction |
+
+This generator turns the polynomial step in Miyazaki, Scott, and Styer,
+[“Handling some Diophantine equation via Euclidean algorithm and its application to purely exponential equations”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18991),
+into an exact witness problem.  The solver receives succinct polynomials
+(A,F\in\mathbb Q[t]) in the paper's own form and must return the unique
+bounded polynomial (W) for which (AP+FW=H) for some (P\in\mathbb Q[t]).
+The checker reduces (FW) modulo (A) with exact arithmetic; it never reads
+the planted answer.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Section 11, equation (11.2), defines the Bézout pair, and Remark 11.1(ii)
+explicitly says polynomial Euclid constructs it.  A generic succinct route—
+binary modular powers, a binary geometric sum, then extended Euclid—runs in
+(O(E^2(\log s+\log N)+E^3)).  At the shipping preset it solved 8/8 instances,
+averaging 16,288 counted exact operations and 0.109 seconds.  The compact route
+notices ((L-1)A=L^E-1), turns the problem into a cyclic coefficient
+permutation, and costs 228 operations.  Thus this is not a Track A hardness
+claim.  The paper also says the (m=2), prime-(q) equation is completely
+solved; the family instead uses the general Section 11 polynomial construction
+with (E\ge3).
+
+## Worked demo (seed 7)
+
+This is the complete rendered instance:
+
+```text
+Find a rational-polynomial Bezout witness.
+
+All polynomials are in Q[t], with exact rational coefficients.  Define the
+distinguished linear polynomial
+
+  L(t) = -6*t + 2.
+
+Its coefficient of t is nonzero, so powers of L form a valid polynomial basis.
+Let E=3, N=13, s=14, k=2,
+c=1, and g=10.  The integers satisfy
+gcd(k,E)=1.  Define
+
+  A(t) = sum of L^j for all integers j from 0 through E-1,
+  I(t) = sum of L^(jE) for all integers j from 0 through N-1,
+  R(t) = sum(r_j L^j),
+  F(t) = c*L^s*(L^k - 1)*I(t) + R(t).
+
+The coefficients r_j of R, listed from degree 0 upward, are
+
+  [-8,0,0,8]
+
+The high-degree polynomial I is specified by the finite geometric-sum formula
+above; it is not an ellipsis standing for unknown data.  All exponents are
+ordinary nonnegative integer exponents.
+
+Find an integer-coefficient polynomial
+
+  W(t) = sum of w_j L^j for all integers j from 0 through 1
+
+such that there exists some P(t) in Q[t] satisfying the exact identity
+
+  A(t)*P(t) + F(t)*W(t) = H,
+
+where H=390.
+
+Equivalently, the exact remainder of FW after division by A must be H.  The
+checker performs that rational-polynomial reduction; P is not part of the
+answer.  The requested representative has degree strictly below deg(A)=
+2, so it is unique.
+
+Bounded answer language: put C=20.  Every degree
+j=0,...,1 must occur exactly once, in increasing order, and w_j must be
+a nonzero integer in the inclusive range -C,...,C.  Use the polynomial rational
+encoding [w_j,1] for each integer, and
+serialize a polynomial term as [[w_j,1],[j]].  Order matters, no degree may
+repeat, and the indexing is 0-based.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as one JSON array of the
+2 polynomial terms just described.
+Example of the exact required shape: <answer>[[[1,1],[0]],[[1,1],[1]]]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `[[[-10,1],[0]],[[-20,1],[1]]]`.
+`verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`; changing the first coefficient
+to `-9` returns `(False, "candidate does not satisfy the Bezout identity modulo A")`.
+This smallest preset is hand-solvable: modulo (A=1+L+L^2), use (L^3=1),
+so (I=13) and the four displayed coefficients of (R) cancel.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | exponent bits `n` | `E` | coefficients | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 3 | 2 | hand example; skipped by hardener |
+| easy | 18 | 11 | 10 | locally passes; oracle call unavailable |
+| medium | 26 | 17 | 16 | locally passes; not reached by oracle |
+| hard | 34 | 23 | 22 | provisional shipping preset; locally passes |
+
+No preset was rejected or held by an oracle.  The OpenRouter key hit its total
+limit before any scored response, so `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY="hard"` remains
+provisional and this directory is **not submission-ready** until Step 4 is
+rerun successfully.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted witnesses verify; 12/12 equal independent Euclid output |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | tagged, fenced model-style response round-trips; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 uniform bounded-language guesses; exact unique-inverse density (5.90\times10^{-261}) for the sampled shipping instance |
+| G5 | generic reference: 16,288 operations, 0.109 s average; demo brute force finds exactly 1/1,600 |
+| G6 | five attacks at 0/8; reference and compact routes each 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubling `n` from 34 to 68 keeps 66 answer atoms and raises the measured reference work |
+| G8 | 100/100 equivalent transformations invariant and valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 513 chars / 129 estimated tokens / 66 atoms; worst-case 136 tokens; compact route 228 operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+| run | preset | scored | result |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 | four retries returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`; no verdict |
+| structural hint | hard | 0/0 | four retries returned the same HTTP 403; no verdict |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0/0 | four retries returned the same HTTP 403; no verdict |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo difference is therefore unavailable, and no conclusion
+about hint sensitivity is justified.  The three transcript files contain the
+script-owned error records rather than fabricated model failures.
+
+## Use
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```python
+from results.2604_18991 import gen_2604_18991 as gen  # or load by file path
+
+params = gen.DIFFICULTY[gen.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = gen.make_instance(seed=123, **params)
+assert gen.verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+Because Python package names cannot normally contain dots or start with digits,
+loading this result by file path is the portable option; the corpus scripts do
+that automatically.  Emit after a successful oracle rerun with:
+
+```bash
+scripts/emit.sh 2604.18991 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This family is easy with a CAS or any polynomial extended-GCD implementation;
+that is the declared Track B reference algorithm.  It also becomes short by
+hand as soon as the solver recognizes the quotient invariant—the intended
+thing being tested.  The G4 probability is uniform over every bounded integer
+coefficient polynomial and is not a posterior over solver strategies.  To
+probe the construction prior, the G6 random restart samples coefficients as
+multiples of the displayed scale `g`; its smaller effective space is still
+(44^{22}\approx1.43\times10^{36}), but only 256 restarts per seed were run.
+The panel did not run a separate Gröbner-basis package because univariate
+extended Euclid is the stronger natural algorithm here.  Most importantly,
+the mandatory four-vendor model evidence is absent due to the external key
+limit; local gates alone do not establish the requested model hardness.
